 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Radial Dual Contouring](17425-radial-dual-contouring.md)
+Radial Dual Contouring (RadialDC) is a method for large-scale world mesh generation that extends Classical Dual Contouring with Dynamic Voxel Sizing and Dynamic Voxel Density Evaluation. It solves the performance bottleneck of generating massive, high-detail terrains by adapting voxel resolution and density function complexity based on the distance from a central player position. Unlike standard Dual Contouring, which uses a uniform grid, RadialDC ensures a crack-free mesh by generating voxels radially outward from a high-detail center, allowing for seamless regeneration as the player moves.
+
+### [Dual Contouring with Root Finding and Analytic Normals](18740-dual-contouring-with-root-finding-and-analytic-normals.md)
+A dual contouring variant that improves feature crispness by using root finding to locate exact surface crossings on edges and computing normals analytically at those points, rather than interpolating or using grid-based approximations. It sets itself apart from its parent [Classical Dual Contouring with Estimated Normals](5802-classical-dual-contouring-with-estimated-normals.md) by replacing estimated or interpolated normals with analytic normals derived from the closest brush point, and from [Radial Dual Contouring](17425-radial-dual-contouring.md) by relying on continuous swept-SDF evaluation rather than radial projection.
+
 ### Dual Contouring Boundary Cap
 A technique to make the Dual Contouring mesher produce a watertight, print-ready solid by capping open boundaries where the surface crosses the sample-cube face, similar to Marching Cubes after boundary sealing [S2, unofficial]. It solves the issue of DC shipping as an interior mesher with open boundaries [S2, unofficial]. Unlike standard DC, which leaves boundaries open, this method ensures the output is a closed solid [S2, unofficial]. It is used in the Fracturing Fog application's `DualContourMesher.Build` and exported via `UserBulbMeshExporter.ExportDualContouring` [S2, unofficial].
-
 *   **Definition:** A boundary sealing technique for DC [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Steps:**
-    1.  March an extra ring of padding cells against a virtual outside shell over the padded cell space $[-1, n]$ [S2, unofficial].
-    2.  For box-face crossings, place cell vertices flush at the face [S2, unofficial].
-    3.  Connect the capped vertices using extended edge-quad loops to form the cap geometry [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Steps:** March an extra ring of padding cells against a virtual outside shell over the padded cell space $[-1, n]$ [S2, unofficial]. For box-face crossings, place cell vertices flush at the face [S2, unofficial]. Connect the capped vertices using extended edge-quad loops to form the cap geometry [S2, unofficial].
 *   **Parameters:** `capBoundary`: A boolean flag controlling whether the boundary cap is applied. Default is true (on) [S2, unofficial].
 *   **Pitfalls:** When the surface is strictly interior (all shell corners are outside), the cap acts as a no-op, producing byte-identical output to the uncapped version [S2, unofficial].
